1:# Generalized symmetries, defects, gauging, and anomalies
9:Fix a spacetime dimension \(d\), a smooth \(d\)-manifold \(M\), and the actual tangential structure of the selected theory: for example, oriented, spin, or a specified structure incorporating spacetime and internal symmetries. State the signature, orientation, time orientation, boundary/collar conventions, and whether the path integral is defined on closed \(M\), on \(M\) with boundary, or on a restricted class of backgrounds. A claim proved on oriented manifolds cannot silently be applied to spin manifolds or to nonorientable manifolds. A Euclidean partition function used to study Lorentzian unitarity also needs its reflection-positivity and continuation assumptions stated.
11:A **defect insertion** is a specified operator or boundary/interface condition supported on a closed embedded submanifold \(\Sigma\hookrightarrow M\), with specified local field data, orientation or framing when required, and permitted junction operators. Its codimension, source/target theories, and insertion into correlation functions must be fixed. Call the defect **topological** only after specifying that its correlators are invariant under allowed isotopies of \(\Sigma\) in the complement of other insertions, with junction data transported coherently. A pictorial crossing or familiar name does not establish topologicality.
17:A **noninvertible symmetry** is encoded by topological defects whose fusion is not group-like. In a finite semisimple example, simple defects \(D_a\) have fusion
25:A **’t Hooft anomaly** is the residual transformation of the partition function under background gauge transformations after all allowed \(d\)-dimensional local counterterms have been considered. This makes the partition function a section of an anomaly line, or equivalently a boundary object relative to an invertible \((d+1)\)-dimensional anomaly theory when such a description is available. It obstructs gauging the global symmetry and is preserved along a symmetry-preserving renormalization-group flow under the stated locality and background-coupling assumptions. It is different from a gauge anomaly of a dynamical gauge redundancy, which obstructs defining the proposed gauge theory unless a specified cancellation mechanism is supplied. It is also different from explicit symmetry breaking, a higher-group background constraint, and a nonconservation equation that is an operator-valued Ward identity rather than a c-number anomaly.
34:Anomaly inflow is a conditional cancellation theorem: the boundary variation and the bulk variation must be evaluated in the same convention on manifolds with boundary, including the same tangential structure and background fields, and their product must be gauge invariant under all admitted transformations. Anomaly matching is likewise conditional: if the exact global symmetry is preserved along the RG flow, its anomaly class is unchanged modulo the declared counterterms. A unique trivially gapped symmetric IR phase is excluded only after the symmetry, anomaly, and allowed topological sectors have been fixed; gapless modes, symmetry breaking, topological order, or a relative boundary theory can match the anomaly.
36:For supersymmetry, distinguish fermionic super-Poincaré generators from internal 0-form symmetries and from higher-form charges. A physical supercharge requires a real Lorentzian form, spinor representation, adjoint operation, common operator domain, Hamiltonian, and a positive-energy Hilbert-space representation. Central charges and brane charges may yield BPS bounds but do not automatically define a generalized global symmetry. An \(R\)-symmetry is an internal global symmetry with its own background/anomaly data; the supercharges themselves are not gaugeable as an ordinary internal group. A supersymmetric anomaly multiplet, supersymmetric inflow action, defect-preserving superalgebra, Witten index, or localization formula needs its own exact model and hypotheses. In particular, a trace index requires trace-class or other controlled spectral hypotheses and a proof that continuum/boundary terms do not invalidate the pairing argument.
44:| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory/qft-c01.md**: **def-qft-framework-units**, **post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement**; **qft-g01.md**: **post-qft-free-photon-model**, **post-qft-qed-local-coupling**; **qft-g02.md**: **post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients**, **post-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-model**, related finite/formal theorems | The selected Minkowski length-coordinate and SI/natural-unit dictionary; a positive transverse free photon model; a smooth classical charged Dirac/Maxwell coupling; and a separate finite graph/formal QED branch. | **post-qft-qed-local-coupling** explicitly uses compactly supported local gauge variations and does not cover large gauge transformations, nontrivial bundles, boundary charges, asymptotic sectors, or an interacting continuum Hilbert space. **qft-g02** does not assert formal-series convergence or a continuum limit. These pages are not suppliers for global anomalies. |
47:| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory-mathematics/qftx-chern-simons-bf-gravity.md**: **def-qftx-cs-bf-actions**, **prop-qftx-cs-bf-variation**, **prop-qftx-three-dimensional-gravity-bf** | A specified Chern–Simons/BF action and its classical variation with boundary terms, plus a three-dimensional gravity/BF interface. | Variation of a classical action does not quantize a theory, establish its quantum anomaly, define a boundary Hilbert space, or prove inflow cancellation. A physical boundary/interface theorem must add the field theory and boundary conditions. |
50:| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory-mathematics/qftx-categorified-line-operators.md** and **qftx-ribbon-braid-representations.md** | A KLR defect-action contract and a categorified braid/Euler interface, alongside a mathematical braid-line model. | These are specific algebraic actions and decategorifications. A mathematical braid representation is not itself a physical topological defect, and its relation to a selected QFT requires a model-specific dictionary and correlator/junction proofs. |
61:| **library/hall-algebras-and-tensor-categories/higher-categorical-symmetry-and-defect-algebras.md**: **def-weak-linear-two-category**, **def-finite-semisimple-two-category**, **def-fusion-two-category**, **def-g-graded-two-vector-space**, **def-four-cocycle-twisted-monoidal-structure-on-2vect-g**, **thm-four-cocycle-2vect-g-is-fusion-two-category**, **thm-three-cochain-gauge-equivalence-2vect-g**, **thm-modc-of-braided-fusion-category-is-fusion-two-category**. | Weak/fusion 2-categories; a finite-group 4-cocycle twisted \(2\mathrm{Vect}_G^\omega\) algebraic model and its cochain gauge equivalence; and an algebraic module-2-category construction. | These algebraic cocycles and obstructions are not automatically physical anomaly classes. The page describes the \(\mathrm{Mod}(\mathcal C)\) construction in a selected 2+1D defect setting and explicitly says it does not classify 3+1D braided fusion 2-category theories. A spacetime dimension, QFT, boundary interpretation, and category-to-defect map must be proved. **Status discrepancy:** this page’s current frontmatter says **status: published** and its cited item carriers, including **def-four-cocycle-twisted-monoidal-structure-on-2vect-g.md** and **thm-four-cocycle-2vect-g-is-fusion-two-category.md**, also say **published**, but its body contains the stale sentence “All carriers remain draft.” Use current carrier frontmatter for item status and do not copy that sentence as status evidence. |
80:| 1 | **aqfts-defects-and-symmetry-basics** / **aqfts-defects-and-symmetry-basics-examples** | Define QFT model/background data and defect insertions; ordinary invertible defect fusion; orientation, duality, isotopy/topologicality, junctions, and the distinction between topological interfaces and self-symmetries. B examples: ordinary group defect, a boundary/interface folding example, and a counterexample where an arbitrary interface is not a symmetry. |
103:| **def-aqft-anomaly-inflow-relative-theory** | Definition; physics | A \(d\)-dimensional relative boundary theory and the matching \((d+1)\)-dimensional bulk invertible theory, in a fixed sign/orientation convention. |
114:| Defect charge/deformation theorem | A precise current equation or an axiomatically supplied topological defect; Stokes’ theorem on an interpolating chain disjoint from charged insertions; contact terms at operator insertions; boundary terms and current improvements; specified support regularity. | A slogan that “conserved current means symmetry” does not prove a topological defect in the quantum model. |
117:| Finite gauging/gluing | The exact finite field groupoid and automorphism weights; cocycle action and triangulation convention; gauge invariance; gluing/sewing; boundary data and orientation signs. | A raw sum over field representatives can overcount gauge-equivalent data or fail to be local under gluing. |
120:| Bulk inflow cancellation | Same backgrounds and cochain/differential conventions on the boundary and bulk; exact variation under all allowed small and large transformations; show bulk-boundary phase cancellation and compatibility with gluing. | A formal local descent cancellation cannot by itself cancel a torsion phase or a defect-junction anomaly. |
121:| Anomaly matching / IR constraint | Prove anomaly-class invariance under the stated RG map; state whether the symmetry is unbroken; specify whether a topological sector or relative boundary theory is admitted. Then rule out only the particular proposed IR realization. | “Anomaly matching forbids a trivial gap” is false without the unbroken-symmetry, locality, gap, degeneracy, and topological-order qualifications. |
133:4. **QFT definition/functoriality:** selected free local algebras and finite BF/RT models exist. General interacting QFT on manifolds with corners/defect strata, background gauge-field dependence, boundary conditions and gluing is not a current universal supplier. Each theorem must name its selected model rather than use “QFT” as a proof assumption with no carrier.
150:| Z.-F. Zhang et al., *Non-invertible Symmetries and Mixed Anomalies from Conserved Current Construction in (3+1)D Twisted BF TQFTs*, arXiv:2601.01523v4 (updated 2026-02-05), INSPIRE 3097775 with no DOI/journal record at this query. | Retrieved 36-page PDF; inspected abstract/introduction PDF pp. 1–4 and summary/outlook pp. 24–26. The scope is a named family of finite-group \(a\wedge a\wedge b\) twisted BF models, where the authors construct invertible and intrinsically noninvertible higher-form symmetry operators from selected currents/projectors, calculate fusion, and distinguish inflow-cancelable mixed anomalies from internal gauging obstructions. Their own outlook lists lattice realizations, boundary theories, higher-group organization, and mixed-state behavior as future directions. Treat as a model-specific recent result; no proof was imported. |
160:3. **Lattice-to-continuum gauging interfaces.** Reconcile movement of finite-regulator constraints/interfaces with continuum background gauge transformations, higher-form symmetry and boundary anomaly inflow. The 2026 lattice interface preprint supplies finite examples, not a universal continuum theorem.

