## Theorem and local-proof obligations

Each item below needs its own complete argument or a named physical postulate/source-backed experiment with no fictitious proof. The table states proof obligations, not established category results.

| Proposed result | Required inputs and proof work | Failure if omitted |
|---|---|---|
| Defect charge/deformation theorem | A precise current equation or an axiomatically supplied topological defect; Stokes’ theorem on an interpolating chain disjoint from charged insertions; contact terms at operator insertions; boundary terms and current improvements; specified support regularity. | A slogan that “conserved current means symmetry” does not prove a topological defect in the quantum model. |
| Finite 2-group background consistency | \(G,A,\rho,\beta\), normalized cocycle, twisted cochains and cup products, explicit degree conventions, and gauge transformations. Verify the cocycle condition, closure, composition, and invariance of \(\delta_a B=a^*\beta\). | Confusing the Postnikov coupling with an anomaly, omitting local coefficients, or using an untwisted \(\delta\) invalidates the background. |
| Categorical fusion/defect realization | The actual QFT or finite lattice Hamiltonian; construction of every defect operator; junction vector spaces; associator maps and pentagon; orientation reversal/adjoints; proof of isotopy invariance and of the claimed fusion coefficients. | Abstract fusion-category axioms or a transfer matrix do not show that any given QFT has those defects. |
| Finite gauging/gluing | The exact finite field groupoid and automorphism weights; cocycle action and triangulation convention; gauge invariance; gluing/sewing; boundary data and orientation signs. | A raw sum over field representatives can overcount gauge-equivalent data or fail to be local under gluing. |
| Perturbative anomaly/counterterm theorem | The selected non-Abelian action, fermion representations, local BRST differential, scheme/regulator and quantum Ward identity; prove the anomaly is a local cocycle and identify allowed counterterms; fix the invariant polynomial and descent normalization. | The current Abelian scalar proposition assumes locality/nilpotence/allowed counterterms and proves only an abstract obstruction equivalence. It does not prove a quantum action principle or identify the physical coefficient. |
| Global anomaly test | A specified gauge group/global form, bundle topology, spin structure, gauge transformation loop/component, fermion determinant/Pfaffian and its regularization; evaluate the resulting phase (eta invariant, index-flow, or explicit determinant line). | Vanishing local \(I_{d+2}\) does not imply absence of a global anomaly; ordinary real Chern–Weil forms miss integral torsion. |
| Bulk inflow cancellation | Same backgrounds and cochain/differential conventions on the boundary and bulk; exact variation under all allowed small and large transformations; show bulk-boundary phase cancellation and compatibility with gluing. | A formal local descent cancellation cannot by itself cancel a torsion phase or a defect-junction anomaly. |
| Anomaly matching / IR constraint | Prove anomaly-class invariance under the stated RG map; state whether the symmetry is unbroken; specify whether a topological sector or relative boundary theory is admitted. Then rule out only the particular proposed IR realization. | “Anomaly matching forbids a trivial gap” is false without the unbroken-symmetry, locality, gap, degeneracy, and topological-order qualifications. |
| Supersymmetric defect anomaly | A physical real superalgebra and preserved subalgebra; exact defect coupling and background supergravity/R fields; a supersymmetric regulator/current multiplet; complete anomaly/inflow computation. | A bare complex superalgebra or non-supersymmetric anomaly polynomial does not furnish a supersymmetric defect theorem. |

For finite bosonic ordinary group-like symmetries, a \(U(1)\)-valued \((d+1)\)-cocycle can define a Dijkgraaf–Witten-type invertible action. That is a clean intermediate result if its triangulated model is fixed. It is not the full anomaly classification for spin theories, continuous groups, higher-form symmetries, nontrivial spacetime structures, or noninvertible defects. For general invertible anomaly theories, use a specified bordism-spectrum/Anderson-dual or differential-cohomology formulation; do not assert that all anomalies are characters of one ordinary bordism group without stating the exact theory category and target.

## Minimal additional prerequisites

The published mathematical library supplies enough to start, but not a complete generalized-anomaly engine. Keep new pure mathematics local to the new category or reuse exact canonical root IDs. Do not create physical dependencies in **items/**.

1. **Higher-group cochains:** ordinary group cohomology and Postnikov towers exist, but the selected twisted 2-group background equation, its local coefficients, cup products, cochain homotopies, Bockstein/Pontryagin-square conventions, and finite gauge-equivalence groupoid need a local explicit contract and proofs. For continuous backgrounds, differential cocycles/characters and their products are a separate missing supplier.
2. **Defect higher categories:** ordinary monoidal/fusion and weak 2-category foundations exist. A physical network on stratified \(d\)-manifolds still needs a chosen defect codimension, labels on each stratum, junction spaces, orientation/dual data, associators and higher coherence. The physical category-to-fusion-data dictionary is not supplied by \(2\mathrm{Vect}_G^\omega\) or \(\mathrm{Mod}(\mathcal C)\) alone.
3. **Global/torsion topology:** ordinary cohomology, oriented bordism of maps, spin structures, Dirac index, characteristic classes, and some Thom-spectrum results exist. A category-level anomaly classification needs the exact spin/orientation structure with a \(BG\) or higher-group background, the corresponding bordism spectrum and computation, and a proof relating its invariants to the physical determinant/anomaly theory. The available oriented \(\Omega_*^{SO}(X)\) item and real Chern–Weil classes are insufficient by themselves.
4. **QFT definition/functoriality:** selected free local algebras and finite BF/RT models exist. General interacting QFT on manifolds with corners/defect strata, background gauge-field dependence, boundary conditions and gluing is not a current universal supplier. Each theorem must name its selected model rather than use “QFT” as a proof assumption with no carrier.
5. **Global supersymmetry:** the complex algebraic N=1 bracket is already defined and checked. Missing physical prerequisites include its chosen real form and charge conjugation, unitary/positive-energy Hilbert representation, common domains/adjoints of supercharges, gauge multiplet and matter-field transformations, auxiliary fields/off-shell closure if claimed, and the selected regularization. Supersymmetric defect/anomaly work additionally needs a precise preserved subalgebra and background fields.

## Current frontier and carefully bounded research questions

The accessible current literature supports several concrete directions, but it does not justify a universal “complete modern classification.” Treat new preprints as research leads until their full text, proof details, version history, journal status and assumptions are checked.

