| Diego Delmastro, Adar Sharon, Yunqin Zheng, *Non-Local Conserved Currents and Continuous Non-Invertible Symmetries*, arXiv:2507.22976v2 (2025-08-15), JHEP 11 (2025) 072 (INSPIRE record 2955822, publication record present). | Retrieved 55-page PDF; read abstract/introduction PDF pp. 1–3 and conclusion/future directions p. 42. The paper’s proven construction is focused on \(1+1\)-dimensional CFTs. Its own open questions include extension to general QFT/higher dimensions, classification of deformations preserving the supporting topological line, the relation of nonlocal currents to quantum-group charges, and background fields for continuous noninvertible symmetries. It says the converse/current characterization is not yet fully understood; this is an explicit frontier lead, not a theorem for arbitrary dimensions. |
| T. D. Brennan, K. Intriligator, *Generalized Families of QFTs*, arXiv:2602.09105v3 (updated 2026-06-09), JHEP 08 (2026) 086 (INSPIRE 3118126, publication record present). | Retrieved 68-page PDF; read abstract and introduction PDF pp. 1–5. The paper develops higher/categorical family anomalies and discusses RG/IR constraints with selected 4D examples. This supplies a recent research direction and candidate exact models, not a proof imported into this scaffold; the derivations and normalizations must be checked if used. |
| David Hofmeier, Giovanna Pimenta, Weiguang Cao, *Lattice Gauging Interfaces and Noninvertible Defects in Higher Dimensions*, arXiv:2605.12601v2 (updated 2026-08-04), INSPIRE 3155176 with no DOI/journal record at this query. | Retrieved 45-page PDF; inspected abstract/introduction PDF pp. 1–5 and conclusion pp. 33–34. The abstract claims explicit \(\mathbb Z_2^{(0)}\) gauging interfaces in 2+1D and \(\mathbb Z_2^{(1)}\) interfaces in 3+1D lattice models, plus movement operators and subgroup-gauging examples. Those are concrete model-specific results under source assumptions, not a general higher-dimensional gauging theorem; the paper’s full construction was not independently reviewed here. |
| Z.-F. Zhang et al., *Non-invertible Symmetries and Mixed Anomalies from Conserved Current Construction in (3+1)D Twisted BF TQFTs*, arXiv:2601.01523v4 (updated 2026-02-05), INSPIRE 3097775 with no DOI/journal record at this query. | Retrieved 36-page PDF; inspected abstract/introduction PDF pp. 1–4 and summary/outlook pp. 24–26. The scope is a named family of finite-group \(a\wedge a\wedge b\) twisted BF models, where the authors construct invertible and intrinsically noninvertible higher-form symmetry operators from selected currents/projectors, calculate fusion, and distinguish inflow-cancelable mixed anomalies from internal gauging obstructions. Their own outlook lists lattice realizations, boundary theories, higher-group organization, and mixed-state behavior as future directions. Treat as a model-specific recent result; no proof was imported. |
| *Realizing Braided Fusion 2-Categories as (3+1)D Mixed-State Topological Orders*, arXiv:2610.03864v1 (2026-10-02), INSPIRE 3211390, with no DOI/journal record at the query. | The current root page **library/hall-algebras-and-tensor-categories/higher-categorical-symmetry-and-defect-algebras.md** records that its abstract and introduction only were read; the proposed mixed-state Ansatz is not proved in those sections and no claim is used as a supplier. The current abstract claims broad realization results. This is an adjacent mixed-state frontier, not evidence that a pure-state 3+1D categorical anomaly classification is complete. |
| Two October 2026 leads in **physics/research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json**: *Surface Defects in the \(\mathcal N=2\) Lens Space Index and Elliptic Integrability*, arXiv:2610.30849 (2026-10-22, INSPIRE 3217942); and *Supersymmetric Observables from Surface Defect Anomalies in 4d and 6d SCFTs*, arXiv:2610.18086 (2026-10-13, INSPIRE 3214070). | The root ledger and direct INSPIRE API responses provide metadata and abstracts only. Neither had a DOI/journal record on 2026-10-23. The first abstract concerns N=2 lens-index surface-defect difference operators and explicitly leaves a general mixed-operator contour prescription open; the second relates 4d/6d supersymmetric observables to defect anomaly polynomials. These are highly relevant source leads for the supersymmetry/defect bridge, not independently checked results or proof suppliers. |

The source dates above are the search/update dates actually observed, not a completeness claim. The root discovery file deliberately retains only metadata and abstracts for its leads. I initially tried the guessed arXiv identifier **2403.12630**; it resolves to an unrelated acoustic-engineering paper and is excluded. The correct 2023 review *ICTP Lectures on (Non-)Invertible Generalized Symmetries* is arXiv:2305.18296v2, but this note does not rely on it because the selected 2026 review and the more narrowly read primary sources above suffice for the stated scope.

## Research questions to preserve as open frontier, not suppliers

1. **Higher-dimensional categorical anomaly classification.** For a fixed unitary fusion 2-category of codimension-one defects in a 3+1D theory, specify which background/junction data are gaugeable modulo local 4D counterterms and whether the complete obstruction is represented by a 4+1D invertible theory for the fixed tangential structure. The finite \(\mathrm{Vec}_G^\omega\) and \(2\mathrm{Vect}_G^\omega\) algebraic models are examples, not a universal answer.
2. **Continuous noninvertible symmetries outside 1+1D CFT.** Extend a precise current/defect construction and prove topologicality, fusion, RG behavior, anomaly/background coupling, and the distinction between intrinsic and “invertible up to gauging” cases. The 2025 article’s conclusion directly identifies higher-dimensional generalization and background coupling as open directions.
3. **Lattice-to-continuum gauging interfaces.** Reconcile movement of finite-regulator constraints/interfaces with continuum background gauge transformations, higher-form symmetry and boundary anomaly inflow. The 2026 lattice interface preprint supplies finite examples, not a universal continuum theorem.
4. **Generalized family anomalies.** Determine when a parameter-space family with explicit symmetry breaking can be treated as a higher/categorical family, and which anomaly data constrain every point of an RG flow or only the family as a whole. The 2026 JHEP paper studies selected models; do not extrapolate its examples without their assumptions.
5. **Supersymmetric defect anomalies.** For a named 4D \(\mathcal N=2\) or 6D \(\mathcal N=(1,0)\) SCFT and a selected half-BPS surface defect, derive the defect anomaly polynomial, supersymmetric background completion, and relation to a Casimir-energy or Rényi observable. The October 2026 arXiv lead’s abstract makes this a timely interface; the full paper and its status must be read before using any formula.
6. **Spin/global anomaly computation.** Build the explicit bridge from a selected chiral QFT determinant line/eta phase to an exact spin-\(G\) bordism-spectrum invariant, including torsion and mixed higher-form backgrounds. The current root spin/Dirac page supplies geometric spin and index prerequisites but not this anomaly map.
7. **3+1D and mixed-state categories.** Separate pure-state QFT anomalies from mixed-state topological orders. The 2026 braided-fusion-2-category realization preprint is a current lead whose body/proofs are not covered here; do not import its abstract as a theorem.

Until a concrete formulation and source/status review exist, these remain research questions rather than **op-** suppliers or claims that a named theorem is false. If the builder mints an **open-problem** item, give it exact hypotheses, current known partial results, date, and a checked source; never make it a prerequisite.

## Integration and validation direction

Use the dependency order: mathematical cochains/categories/bordism; definitions of the exact defect/background model; selected QFT postulates and unit/field data; local anomaly or finite gauging arguments; global anomaly/inflow; then anomaly matching and IR applications. Physical items must declare framework, physical scope, dependency roles, assumptions, units, and source/empirical status. Pure mathematical items must have only mathematical dependencies. Postulates adopt the chosen model and are not derived from symmetry algebra; experiments report evidence and uncertainty but are not needed for the present symmetry scaffold.

The new category can reuse the QFT free/photon and finite-regulator pages, local finite BF construction, and mathematical categorical/cohomological suppliers as explicit dependencies. It must not claim a nonperturbative continuum QFT from those models, a physical realization from an abstract category, a global-anomaly absence from a vanishing local polynomial, or an anomaly cancellation from a representation trace alone. New proofs, source retrievals, and frontier status checks belong to their exact future item obligations. This note reports research source reading only; it confers no review receipt, readiness, workflow-gate result, or publication permission.


