4. **Categorical/noninvertible anomalies** depend on defect fusion, associators, junctions, and background or gauging data for that exact higher category. Do not label every obstruction to a chosen gauging as an anomaly field theory until the local and global obstruction, counterterm equivalence, and possible inflow have all been specified.

Anomaly inflow is a conditional cancellation theorem: the boundary variation and the bulk variation must be evaluated in the same convention on manifolds with boundary, including the same tangential structure and background fields, and their product must be gauge invariant under all admitted transformations. Anomaly matching is likewise conditional: if the exact global symmetry is preserved along the RG flow, its anomaly class is unchanged modulo the declared counterterms. A unique trivially gapped symmetric IR phase is excluded only after the symmetry, anomaly, and allowed topological sectors have been fixed; gapless modes, symmetry breaking, topological order, or a relative boundary theory can match the anomaly.

For supersymmetry, distinguish fermionic super-Poincaré generators from internal 0-form symmetries and from higher-form charges. A physical supercharge requires a real Lorentzian form, spinor representation, adjoint operation, common operator domain, Hamiltonian, and a positive-energy Hilbert-space representation. Central charges and brane charges may yield BPS bounds but do not automatically define a generalized global symmetry. An \(R\)-symmetry is an internal global symmetry with its own background/anomaly data; the supercharges themselves are not gaugeable as an ordinary internal group. A supersymmetric anomaly multiplet, supersymmetric inflow action, defect-preserving superalgebra, Witten index, or localization formula needs its own exact model and hypotheses. In particular, a trace index requires trace-class or other controlled spectral hypotheses and a proof that continuum/boundary terms do not invalidate the pairing argument.

