+# Batch 36 — Step 1 authoring evidence
+
+Run: `advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23`  
+Scope: `qftx-defects-and-topological-operators` and its examples companion.
+
+## Scope and plan reconciliation
+
+Read the batch task, generic beta scaffold brief, run owner direction, the complete assigned design section in `research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md`, the corresponding `research/plan-spec.json` rows, the exact proposed item contracts, and the relevant specialist note `research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md`.
+
+The canonical plan row and assigned design agree on the A/B IDs, title, category, physics library, order 992, companion, and required pages `qftx-defect-fusion-mathematics` and `qftx-susy-algebra-and-multiplets`. The plan has `items: []`; the design and proposed inventory name the two exact A-page definitions `def-aqft-topological-defect` and `def-aqft-topological-interface`. Per the binding owner direction, the empty plan arrays are authoring placeholders, so the two specified A items are entered in the batch manifest. The same direction requires substantive B-page examples, so this batch adds two finite-model examples and one counterexample on the assigned B page. This fills only batch 36's pair; the shared plan remains untouched.
+
+The assigned design scope is preserved: define embedded defects and interfaces with an isotopy-invariance test, distinguish endodefects from interfaces between different theories, require invertibility and observable preservation for an internal symmetry, and do not infer physical realization from categorical fusion data or BPS algebra. The interface definition cites the earlier in-pair defect definition because it reuses its correlator and isotopy criterion; this is the one additional item edge beyond the proposed empty `proposed_deps` fields. Its level is therefore recomputed from the actual edge.
+
+## Dependency inventory and inspected suppliers
+
+The recorded page-level dependency level is 3: batch 36 requires batches 15 (level 0) and 29 (level 2). The item graph after authoring is expected to be:
+
+| Item | In-run item suppliers | Level |
+|---|---|---:|
+| `def-aqft-topological-defect` | none | 0 |
+| `def-aqft-topological-interface` | `def-aqft-topological-defect` | 1 |
+| `ex-aqft-z2-topological-defect-finite-quantum-mechanics` | `def-aqft-topological-defect` | 1 |
+| `ex-aqft-invertible-interface-between-finite-quantum-mechanics` | `def-aqft-topological-interface` | 2 |
+| `cex-aqft-topological-interface-not-symmetry` | `def-aqft-topological-interface` | 2 |
+
+`def-aqft-noninvertible-defect-fusion` on `qftx-defect-fusion-mathematics` is a current draft definition of finite semisimple fusion-category and semistrict fusion-2-category data. Its exact definition expressly says these abstract categories do not construct QFT defect operators, specify spacetime support, or prove topologicality. Its current Step 1 record is `ready`; its exact mathematical premises are the read-only published definitions `def-fusion-and-multifusion-category` and `def-monoidal-category`. The batch 36 items do not treat this abstract category as proof of a physical realization.
+
+The required `qftx-susy-algebra-and-multiplets` page currently contains `pthm-qftx-n2-bps-saturation-kernel-equivalence` and `def-qftx-bps-state-and-saturation`. Both current Step 1 records are `escalated`. The theorem's actual conditional result and complete proof were inspected: it is restricted to a massive rest fiber with metric `(-,+,+,+)`, `epsilon_12=1`, natural units `hbar=c=1`, scalar central charge, actual Hilbert adjoints and a common product domain, and `AC_omega`; it proves an algebraic saturation/kernel equivalence, not existence or topologicality of a physical defect. The definition's current escalation follows that theorem. The theorem record identifies the unresolved page-level inputs precisely: four `aqft-susy-math-foundations` item records and all four `qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model` item records are missing. The required page edge therefore remains `open`; these definitions and examples make no supersymmetry, BPS, or defect-realization claim. Draft status and precheck status alone were not treated as gaps.
+
+## Full-text sources inspected
+
+1. Davide Gaiotto, Anton Kapustin, Nathan Seiberg, and Brian Willett, [*Generalized Global Symmetries*](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.5148), arXiv:1412.5148v2. Inspected the complete relevant introduction passage, PDF pp. 1–3, including the definition of codimension-`q+1` defect operators, deformation invariance of their correlators, group fusion, and the authors' operator/defect convention. This supports the physical isotopy criterion; it does not establish an arbitrary continuum QFT realization or a general interface fusion limit.
+
+2. Nils Carqueville, [*Lecture Notes on 2-Dimensional Defect TQFT*](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05747), arXiv:1607.05747. Read the complete relevant §2.1, PDF pp. 5–8: oriented 2D defect bordisms, region labels, source/target maps for domain walls, defect isotopy classes, and the symmetric monoidal functor assigning correlators. The notes explicitly specialize the detailed construction to oriented 2D TQFTs and say details for dimensions above three are not worked out; no general higher-dimensional construction is imported here.
+
+Both are retained as source-qualified treatments for the A page, with a full lecture-note treatment included. The A definitions state the model data and criteria directly; the cited texts supply context and do not replace local definitions. No source retrieval failure occurred in the inspected browser retrievals. `source-fetch-check --stamp` and its actual result are still pending.
+
+## Item progress
+
+- `def-aqft-topological-defect` is authored as an uninterrupted definition of a model-bound insertion and exact isotopy-invariance predicate. It specifies the manifold, field/configuration data, regularity, boundary conditions, correlator functional, support, local labels, junction data, allowed isotopies, and unit/normalization boundary. It makes no QFT existence or realization claim. It has no item-level supplier; its level is 0. References: Gaiotto et al., §1, PDF pp. 1–3, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.5148; Carqueville, §2.1, PDF pp. 5–8, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05747. Formatting and render checks are pending. Next action: author the codimension-one interface definition as a consumer of this exact defect criterion.
+- `def-aqft-topological-interface` is now authored with `def-aqft-topological-defect` as an explicit formulation prerequisite (level 1). It states exact source/target model compatibility, a cooriented collar, one-sided transmission data, the inherited isotopy test, a fusion limit that is asserted only when all declared correlators converge, and the additional two-sided inverse and observable-preservation conditions for an internal symmetry. The small `tikzcd` records only source/target direction and is not used as proof. It makes no BPS, supersymmetry, or general continuum-fusion claim. References: Gaiotto et al., §1, PDF pp. 1–3, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.5148; Carqueville, §2.1, PDF pp. 5–8, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.05747. Formatting and render checks are pending. Next action: author positive finite-model examples on the B page.
+- `ex-aqft-z2-topological-defect-finite-quantum-mechanics` is authored as a finite $\mathbb C^2$ model with $H=0$, natural units $\hbar=1$, all matrix observables, and the explicit nontrivial unitary $\mathbb Z_2$ representation. Its verification proves the representation law, nontrivial action, and correlator independence under every allowed motion that does not cross another insertion. It depends only on the defect definition (level 1), and makes no continuum or empirical claim. References provide literature context; the matrix checks are local. Formatting and render checks are pending. Next action: author a nontrivial invertible interface between two explicitly related finite models.
+- `ex-aqft-invertible-interface-between-finite-quantum-mechanics` is authored for two finite $\mathbb C^2$ models with $H_-=\operatorname{diag}(0,\Delta)$, $H_+=\operatorname{diag}(\Delta,0)$, and $V$ the swap matrix. The proof checks the unitary and Hamiltonian intertwining identities, derives the exponential identity from its convergent matrix series, proves position independence including fixed matched insertions, and exhibits the inverse composites. It depends on the interface definition (level 2) and explicitly distinguishes an equivalence interface from an internal symmetry. No continuum or empirical claim is made. Formatting and render checks are pending. Next action: write the counterexample that a topological interface need not be an internal symmetry.
+- `cex-aqft-topological-interface-not-symmetry` is authored as a direct counterexample with source space $\mathbb C$, target space $\mathbb C^2$, zero Hamiltonians, and injection $J(z)=(z,0)$. The complete allowed correlators are position-independent, while dimension/rank rules preclude a two-sided inverse and the source/target models differ. This refutes the universal statement that every topological interface is an internal symmetry. It depends on the interface definition (level 2). The exact finite model and all required examples are now assigned to the B companion.
+
+## Final validation, coverage, and readiness
+
+The final batch manifest assigns two A definitions and three B examples/counterexamples. The A definitions are the two exact proposed contracts; the B entries set `inventory_expansion_required: true` to record the owner-directed substantive examples. The actual in-batch item levels are `0, 1, 1, 2, 2`. A scoped recomputation reports no item-level errors; the current page graph gives batches 15/29/36 levels `0/2/3`, respectively, with no cycles.
+
